╭─────────────────────────────── Round 1 · CRITIC (Conf: 0.75, Ready: False) ────────────────────────────────╮
│ • [C1 -> A2] (CHALLENGED): Ranking by most recent Engagement date and active Mission linkage will surface  │
│ people who have left their post. Example: a ministry liaison who attended three recent meetings resigns    │
│ after a cabinet reshuffle. Nobody has logged a new engagement with the replacement, so the departed person │
│ stays ranked #1 with a fresh-looking 'why this person' justification. Staff then send sensitive            │
│ correspondence to a former official. Because last_verified is only updated by logging an Engagement, it    │
│ also marks a departed person as recently verified. It measures activity, not whether the person still      │
│ holds the role. Also, no cleanup of inactive flags or duplicate contacts is planned, so data quality       │
│ across 100 countries is unproven.                                                                          │
│   Eval: The Proposer admits in A5 that turnover is not detected and offers only a stale badge. The         │
│ 12-month threshold is far too long for ministerial turnover. The ranking also presents confident 'why this │
│ person' output on data that may be wrong.                                                                  │
│ • [C2 -> A4] (CHALLENGED): Inheriting existing Country/Mission record-level access is not enough once      │
│ contacts are aggregated into a derived panel, global search, and a 'who do I contact' query. Today a user  │
│ may see a Country record without being able to browse its contacts, or may see a Mission without seeing    │
│ every contact linked to it. A new ranked cross-record view, and the justification text (Mission/Project    │
│ names, last engagement dates), can expose the existence of confidential missions or engagement history to  │
│ staff who lack access to the underlying Mission or Engagement. Global search is the highest-risk path      │
│ because it is often built on an index that bypasses record-level filters. A coordinator who can see        │
│ Country X but not a confidential Mission Y could infer Y's existence and the named contact from the 'why   │
│ this person' line. The 'zero instances in UAT' criterion cannot verify this unless it explicitly tests the │
│ derived panel, search index, and justification strings.                                                    │
│   Eval: The Proposer asserts that permissions are inherited but does not say how the derived panel, the    │
│ search index, and the justification text enforce per-record and per-field access.                          │
│ • [C3 -> A3] (CHALLENGED): The Proposer treats recency of engagement as a proxy for the 'right' contact.   │
│ In practice the most-engaged contact is often a junior desk officer or an administrative assistant, while  │
│ the decision-maker is engaged rarely. Rule (1) also lets any contact linked to an active Mission outrank   │
│ everyone else, even if the link is stale (a Mission closed on paper but still marked active). A            │
│ coordinator following the top-3 list for an urgent or sensitive matter reaches the wrong level and may     │
│ bypass the correct ministry channel, a diplomatic misstep. There is also no handling of ties, contacts     │
│ with no engagement history, or a country with more than three valid contacts. The 80% survey criterion is  │
│ weak: it measures agreement with what users would have done anyway, not whether the contact was correct.   │
│   Eval: A6 defers protocol and seniority to humans, but the panel is labelled 'Recommended Contact' with a │
│ ranked list and no caveat. Users will treat it as authoritative, and fatigue sets in once the top          │
│ suggestion proves wrong a few times. Staff then ignore it.                                                 │
│ • [C4 -> A5] (CHALLENGED): The stale badge will fire on a large share of contacts, because last_verified   │
│ updates only when an Engagement is logged. In a CRM covering ~100 countries, many valid contacts (for      │
│ example, a stable head of a ministry department with only occasional contact) will show 'stale' after 12   │
│ months, while departed officials with recent engagement show no badge. Once a large proportion of records  │
│ carry the badge, staff will learn to ignore it, which is the alert-fatigue failure. The badge also never   │
│ prompts action, since no verification workflow exists and no one owns the follow-up. It also does not      │
│ account for our own coordinator turnover: when a Regional Coordinator leaves, their engagement logging     │
│ stops and every contact they covered ages into stale together.                                             │
│   Eval: The Proposer treats the badge as an 'honest signal', but it is a noisy signal with no owner and no │
│ way to clear it except by logging an unrelated engagement. This is a solvable engineering gap, for example │
│ a lightweight one-click 'confirm still current' action or a 'left post' flag that removes the contact from │
│ ranking.                                                                                                   │
│                                                                                                            │
